As shown in Fig. 3, SepaCVAE uses $G(\cdot)$ to separate the contexts into different groups. For the one-to-many phenomenon, the contexts in different groups will have different prior distributions $p(z|G_{*}(\cdot))$ , which is easily affected by the different posterior distributions. As for the many-to-one phenomenon, SepaCVAE makes the contexts $(c_{1}, c_{2})$ generate latent variables related to the response $r_{1}$ only when it contains group information $G_{1}(\cdot)$ . The other group would help the contexts to align with the other latent variables.

# 4.2 Dialogue augmentation

